-- --tool=callgrind --dump-instr=yes --main-stacksize=1073741824 --max-stackframe=1073741824 (the machine
-- otherwise re-executes itself through /proc/self/exe to raise its stack, which under valgrind is the
-- tool, not the machine; /usr/bin/valgrind is a wrapper that cannot pass this); callgrind_annotate
-- --inclusive=no for self cost, --tree=both for callers, and --auto=yes with a source file for lines.
-- About 60 times slower than the machine: Brunerie 4 min, lib/library.cub and lib/views.cub over an
-- hour each, or a partial profile at any point with callgrind_control -d.
-- Known (2026-11-16, at src/big.c and src/lang.c, tree at 538a70fa): measured, fixed. Shares of
-- instructions in lib/views.cub (a partial profile, 154 billion), lib/library.cub (partial, 200
-- billion) and Brunerie (whole, 24 billion): wn_parse 9.0%, 5.2%, 2.7%: compile reads every word
-- literal's digits (S_WORD), and wn_parse made r·10 + d as ten wn_adds per digit, each a calloc and a
-- free (in lib/views.cub 34M additions, 3.4M digits); it now multiplies in place, one 64-bit word up to
-- 19 digits (10^19 < 2^64), the limbs otherwise (proof at wn_parse; the old and new functions agree on
-- 244,007 digit strings of 0 to 60 digits, the 64-bit boundary included). lookup_i's scan of the
-- binder stack 5.5%, 3.1%, 3.9%, almost all from maxlev: every pop of any binder bumped the epoch, after
-- which each name's next lookup scanned the whole stack; now each binder keeps its name's previous
-- header (prev) and every pop gives it back (pop_binds), so the header is always the topmost binder
-- and no lookup scans (the invariant and its proof at lookup_i; lift pushes its saved binders again
-- rather than copying them over the stack). Results at 433656b4, one worker, uncached, the machines
-- run alternately with nothing else running: Brunerie 5.1 s to 4.3 s, lib/library.cub 60.6 s to
-- 54.7 s, lib/views.cub 157.2 s to 149.9 s, workflow/hyperactivity.cub 108.2 s to 102.1 s; every
-- output identical byte for byte (the library's 2511 passed and 24 failed, the 24 being
-- lib/derive.cub's unsigned definitions, before and after), with CUB_CHKBIND=1 checking every
-- lookup against the scan in all four runs. The time gained is less than the instructions removed:
-- instructions are not cycles, and the allocator's and the key table's costs are memory, which
-- callgrind does not time.
