-- 2026-11-18 (Avik, 01:49: "do you think it's acceptable to either of us that the evaluator is that
-- slow?"; 01:50: "use a proper profiling tool like perf find something that works"; 02:37: "Derive the
-- big O. Fitting is stupid. Measuring is stupid."; 02:53: "we don't allow any tech debt, anything you
-- see you must make perfect ... assume hyperactivity means lightspeed"). Profiled with callgrind
-- (valgrind is installed; run with ulimit -s unlimited, CUBICAL_STACKED=1, CUBICAL_WORKERS=1). The
-- problems found, each with its cost from the code:
--  1. Every load wrote and re-read the names log, finding each line's name by a scan: Θ(lines ×
--     names), 4.4 of 9.5 billion instructions a start. Done (f28e0d50): a hash, Θ(lines); a start is
--     5.0 billion (1.2 s).
--  2. Every load compiles every loaded definition again (compile_bound, 94% of what is left of a
--     start): Θ(program) a process; the compiled form kept under the definition's key makes it Θ(changed).
--  3. Every built value is keyed at run time: a call hashes each argument (bind: key_of_port), each
--     lambda, application, pair, constructor, case or let built in a body is hashed from its syntax and
--     its slots' keys (synkey, cached a frame), and each deferred operation with a key is entered in a
--     global sharded table under a lock (memo_register) and looked up when it runs (memo_begin), so
--     equal computations anywhere run once. Over streaming text nothing is shared (65 hits in 3.3
--     million steps); CUBICAL_NOMEMO=1 made a 1 MB run 23% faster with the keys still computed. The
--     keys are owed only where an equal computation can meet another; which subterms those are is
--     decided from the compiled body.
--  4. Every frame allocates (run_frame: calloc of supplies, kc_enter), every cell is malloc'd and
--     freed, the memo shards are locked with one worker: per-thread pools.
--  An interaction costs about 1,400 instructions (5.17 billion for 3.68 million), against tens for an
--  interaction net run lean; items 3 and 4 are that difference.
-- In the library and the programs: h-prefix s, a partial application, is rebuilt at every position of
-- a scan, Θ(n·k) for Θ(k); h-split goes through a reversed accumulator, two passes for one; matching is
-- naive, Θ(ℓ·k) for KMP's Θ(ℓ + k); /chat's search is a binary search of full scans, Θ(n·|t|·log|t|) a
-- query, for a suffix automaton of the record (Θ(n) once, Θ(|t| + occ + output) a query), which also
-- takes grep out of lib/meaning.cub. The automaton is built in the machine, so it waits on 3 and 4.
-- Item 3, done (2026-11-18, 0bace3c2; Avik: "two separate machines unaware of eachother same
-- computation, of course not shared"): the memo and every key are gone, not limited to where equal
-- computations can meet, because no computation names another program-wide: sharing is a duplicator
-- or a box a reference holds. The library 64 s to 47 s and the workflow 121 s to 59 s, every answer the
-- same. A8's cut of a substitution to a box's free dimensions stays, local to the box. Item 1 of the
-- library list is done too (8e3a5a1e: h-split in one pass, h-prefix built once a line). /chat's search:
-- no whole-record automaton either (memory-is-local); the occurrences are found piece by piece.
evaluator-speed : Node
evaluator-speed = node "The machine's time goes to the language's interactions: no search by name and no repeated walk grows with the program or the library." (cons language-implementation-remainder nil)
